#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078220 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df6ae15d-7005-5a93-9432-c4814dc9573d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given the Pattern and the Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "781f5c90-7d84-554a-9131-efc9f079d16a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4203" :question/problem "Calculate the number of terms in the following arithmetic series:\n$5+7+9+\\cdots = 140$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_{N} = \\dfrac{N}{2} \\big(2a_1+(N-1)d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe are given the sum $S_N = 140$, and the first term $a_1=5$. Our goal is to find $N$, the number of terms.\n\nThe common difference $d$ can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= a_{2} - a_{1} \\\\\n&= 7 - 5 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= \\frac{N}{2}(2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) \\\\\n140 &= \\frac{N}{2}(2(5) + (N - 1)(2)) \\\\\n140 &= \\frac{N}{2}(10 + 2N - 2) \\\\\n140 &= \\frac{N}{2}(2N + 8) \\\\\n140 &= N^{2} + 4N \\\\\n0 &= N^{2} + 4N - 140\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN &= (-4 ± \\sqrt{4^{2} - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-140)})/(2 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-4 ± \\sqrt{576}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-4 ± 24}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $N=10$ or $N=-14$. We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be positive.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=10$ terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078221 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b23f178-4161-56d3-98e0-00b3181417e2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms and the Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "ca02b0a6-5fe1-55da-9c59-844999c2a160" :question/math-academy-id "e-4204" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has the $5$th term $a_{5}=14$, the $7$th term $a_{7}=19$, and the sum of the series is $S_N=850$. Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series:\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac N 2 \\big(2a_1 + (N-1) d \\big)\n$$\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd &= \\frac{a_{7} - a_{5}}{7 - 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{19 - 14}{7 - 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2} \\\\\n&= 2.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $a_{5}=14$. We can compute $a_1$ using the formula for the $n$th term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\na_{5} &= a_{1} + (5 - 1) \\cdot 2.5 \\\\\n14 &= a_{1} + 4 \\cdot 2.5 \\\\\n14 &= a_{1} + 10 \\\\\na_{1} &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $a_1=4$, $d=2.5$, and $S_N=850$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= \\frac{N}{2}(2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) \\\\\n850 &= \\frac{N}{2}(2(4) + (N - 1)(2.5)) \\\\\n850 &= \\frac{N}{2}(8 + 2.5N - 2.5) \\\\\n850 \\cdot 2 &= N(5.5 + 2.5N) \\\\\n1700 &= 2.5N^{2} + 5.5N \\\\\n2.5N^{2} + 5.5N - 1700 &= 0 \\\\\n5N^{2} + 11N - 3400 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the equation above, the quadratic formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN &= (- (11) ± \\sqrt{(-11)^{2} - 4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot (-3400)})/(2 \\cdot 5) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-11 ± \\sqrt{68121}}{10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-11 ± 261}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $N=25$ or $N=-27.2$. We disregard the negative solution since $N$ must be a positive integer.\n\nTherefore, our series has $N=25$ terms."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078222 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "822ee6ad-d15c-518d-b798-1aa2ba84ca20" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Using the Formula for the Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "badf4823-0460-5e11-a58b-e817c80416d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4205" :question/problem "The first term of an arithmetic series is $a_1=2$, the common difference is $d=4$, and the sum of the series is $S_N=200$. Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/worked-solution "To find the number of terms, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac N 2 \\big(2a_1 + (N-1) d \\big)\n$$\n\nWe substitute $a_1=2$, $d=4$, and $S_N=200$ into the above and solve for $N$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= \\frac{N}{2}(2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) \\\\\n200 &= \\frac{N}{2}(2 \\cdot 2 + (N - 1) \\cdot 4) \\\\\n200 &= \\frac{N}{2}(4 + 4N - 4) \\\\\n200 &= \\frac{N}{2}(4N) \\\\\n200 &= 2N^{2} \\\\\n100 &= N^{2} \\\\\nN &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have taken the positive square root because $N$ can't be negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b23f178-4161-56d3-98e0-00b3181417e2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Terms in an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fce20eb-f209-59d8-81ff-177df8dc45fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077422} {:answer/id #uuid "fdda9447-7ba4-55f0-93ed-75e96a872439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077423} {:answer/id #uuid "43af3c56-f5d5-51d0-9a7a-9e4290a8b1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077424} {:answer/id #uuid "5642cb7f-3598-570f-96ee-60cbd17492f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077425} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc0c5b1-77dd-5404-9865-b23e5162dfe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fce20eb-f209-59d8-81ff-177df8dc45fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077422} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c8d7cff-adad-57df-899a-ef4ec0ce90df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077421}] :question/id #uuid "71cd7c6d-9bf1-5897-a501-31a80738abbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-5446" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nAn arithmetic series has the first term $a_{1} = 4$, the $8$th term $a_{8} = 60$, and the sum of the series is $S_{N} = 1600$. Find the number of terms $N$ in the series." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
